{
  "image_name": "42c12962f76b7341a9cafc0108281251.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three advanced oxidation routes for degrading contaminants and shows that they all work by generating highly reactive oxygen species that convert pollutants into intermediates and, ultimately, mineralized products such as CO2 and H2O.\n\nOn the left, chemical-mediated AOPs are divided into homogeneous and heterogeneous reactions. In the homogeneous case, dissolved metal catalysts cycle through oxidation states to activate hydrogen peroxide, ozone, or persulfate, producing radicals such as hydroxyl and sulfate radicals. The heterogeneous version shows similar chemistry occurring on a solid catalyst surface, with electron transfer at the surface helping generate the same oxidizing species.\n\nThe middle schematic summarizes photocatalysis. Under ultraviolet or visible light, a semiconductor-like photocatalyst creates electron–hole pairs. Some carriers recombine, but the useful ones react with oxygen and water to form superoxide- and hydroxyl-type species that attack pollutants.\n\nThe right panel shows electrocatalysis driven by direct current between anode and cathode. It distinguishes direct electro-oxidation at the electrode from indirect oxidation, where precursor molecules like water, hydrogen peroxide, ozone, or persulfate are activated to form radicals. Overall, the figure emphasizes different energy inputs—chemical, light, and electricity—but a common oxidative degradation mechanism.",
